<h2>The case in brief</h2>
<p>The applicant had been in judicial custody since 23 March 2026 in connection with Case Crime No. 108 of 2026, registered at Police Station Bazpur, District Udham Singh Nagar, for offences which the order records under Sections 341, 354-D, 376 and 506 of the Indian Penal Code. The matter came before Hon&rsquo;ble Mr. Justice Alok Mahra on a bail application numbered BA1/925/2026, and was decided on 17 November 2026.</p>
<p>What distinguished this application from a routine plea was the state of the trial record. By the time the matter was heard, the trial court had examined the complainant — the victim&rsquo;s brother — as PW-1 and the victim herself as PW-2. The order records that both witnesses denied the incident, did not support the prosecution, and denied the statements attributed to them during the investigation.</p>

<h2>What the Court held</h2>
<p>The Court looked at the record and found that the testimony of the two witnesses, as recorded before the trial court, did not lend support to the prosecution version at that stage. On that basis it held that the applicant had made out a case for bail, and directed his release on a personal bond with two sureties of like amount to the satisfaction of the court concerned. The Court expressly recorded that it was not expressing any opinion on the merits of the case — a standard and important caveat, because the trial court must still evaluate the full evidence unburdened by anything said at the bail stage.</p>

<h2>Why stage-of-trial matters in bail law</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>At arrest.</strong> The court sees only the FIR, the investigation material and the seriousness of the allegation. In offences like Section 376 IPC, that weighs heavily toward custody.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>At chargesheet.</strong> The investigation is complete; custodial interrogation is no longer a factor. Courts begin asking what continued detention actually serves.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>At evidence.</strong> Once material witnesses are examined, the court has something better than police papers: sworn testimony. If that testimony does not support the prosecution, the foundation for custody erodes.</div>
</div>
<p>This progression explains why successive bail applications are permissible on changed circumstances. The question is never whether an earlier rejection was wrong, but whether the case now stands differently. The examination of the victim and the complainant — and their refusal to support the case — is among the clearest changes a record can show.</p>

<h2>The practical lessons</h2>
<p>For the defence, the order underlines the value of timing. A bail application filed the week before material witnesses are examined asks the court to speculate; one filed the week after asks the court to read a transcript. Where the prosecutrix and the first informant have both resiled, the transcript usually speaks for itself. Defence counsel should place certified copies of the depositions on record and frame the application squarely on the change in circumstances, rather than re-arguing grounds already rejected.</p>
<p>For the prosecution and complainants, the case is a reminder that maintenance of the charge depends on witnesses who stand by their statements. Where witnesses are won over or worn down, the State&rsquo;s remedy lies in the tools the law provides during trial — not in opposing bail for custody&rsquo;s own sake.</p>
<div class="note">This article reports a judicial order for general information. It does not comment on any pending trial, and bail outcomes always depend on the specific record of each case.</div>
